---

<script lang="ts">
	import { InstallationTabs } from "$site"
</script>

```typescript
import { sms } from "postboi"

await sms({ to: "+447788223344", message: "Your code is 4291" })
```

Same shape as [`mail()`](/quick-start): the provider and its credentials come from your
environment, [hooks](/hooks) run around every send, and failures throw a normalized
[`PostboiError`](/errors).

<InstallationTabs exec args="init --sms" />

## What a send answers

A send resolves once the provider has taken the text, with the same four fields whichever
provider took it, beside that provider's own:

```typescript
const sent = await sms({ to: "+447788223344", message: "Your code is 4291" })
sent.id // the provider's id for it, the one its receipts name
sent.ids // one per recipient where the provider makes several, else [id]
sent.provider // "twilio", or whichever in a chain took it
sent.delivery // "queued", "scheduled", "sent", "delivered" or "unknown"
```

`delivery` is where the text stood when the provider answered, not whether it arrived:
that comes later, through [a delivery receipt](#delivery-receipts). So a
[failover chain](#failing-over) or a change of provider reads the same `id` and `delivery`,
and the provider's own fields (`sid` on Twilio, `message_uuid` on Vonage) are still there.

The first question is **where you're sending**, because unlike email the right SMS provider
depends on the destination: a UK-native provider is materially cheaper into the UK and no
use anywhere else. Your answer also becomes the default country, which is how national
numbers like `07788 223344` get resolved.

## Phone numbers

Anything unambiguous works without configuration:

```typescript
await sms({ to: "+447788223344", message: "…" }) // international
await sms({ to: "00447788223344", message: "…" }) // 00 prefix
await sms({ to: ["+447788223344", "+353871234567"], message: "…" })
await sms({ to: "+447788223344, +353871234567", message: "…" }) // comma-separated
```

National formats need a country, either as a default or per send:

```typescript
// postboi.config.ts → sms.default.country, or POSTBOI_SMS_COUNTRY
await sms({ to: "07788 223344", message: "…" }) // → +447788223344
await sms({ to: "07788 223344", message: "…", country: "GB" }) // per send
```

Give it an ISO country code (`"GB"`) or a dialling code (`"+44"`). The dialling code always
works, including for countries the ISO table doesn't list.

### Numbers, and why they're risky

A bare number reads nicely and is accepted:

```typescript
await sms({ to: 447788223344, message: "…" })
```

But a JavaScript number **cannot carry a leading `+` or a leading `0`**, so `07788 223344`
becomes `7788223344` and nothing downstream can tell a UK number from a US one. We resolve
what we safely can and **throw rather than guess** otherwise:

```typescript
await sms({ to: "7788223344", message: "…" })
// PostboiError: Cannot tell what country "7788223344" belongs to.
// Write it in full international form ("+447788223344"), or set a default
// country via POSTBOI_SMS_COUNTRY or `sms.default.country` in postboi.config.
```

A wrong guess texts a stranger, so there isn't a silent fallback. **Pass `+`-prefixed
strings and none of this applies.**

### Keeping numbers out of your logs

A phone number is personal data, and an error tracker is the place it most often leaks to:
a provider's refusal names the number it refused, and an `on.error` hook forwards it. Turn
on `redact` and SMS keeps numbers out of everything that leaves a send for somewhere else:

```typescript
export default config({ sms: { redact: true } }) // or POSTBOI_SMS_REDACT=1
```

A thrown error's message, stack and `raw` are masked (`+447700900123` becomes
`+4*********23`), and so is the message and response that `after.send` and `on.error` are
shown. The class, `code`, `status` and `reason` are untouched, so handling an error works
the same, and the text itself still goes to the real number. `before.send` sees the real
message, since what it returns is what gets sent. `redact: true` on one provider's
constructor does it for that provider alone. For your own logging, `redact_phones(text)`
masks every run of seven digits or more in a string, and `mask_phone(number)` masks one.

## Development sends nothing

In development, texts are **captured and logged, never sent**, even with a fully
configured provider:

```
postboi (mock sms): +447788223344
  from: POSTBOI
  cost: 1 segment (gsm7)

Your code is 4291
```

This is stricter than email, where the [dev inbox](/dev-inbox) only intercepts when
it's actually running. The asymmetry is deliberate: a stray email is embarrassing, a stray
text costs money, reaches a real handset, and cannot be recalled.

When you genuinely need real delivery locally:

```bash
POSTBOI_SMS_DEV=send
```

```typescript
// or, permanently, in postboi.config.ts
export default config({ dev: { sms: false } })
```

## Sender

Most providers need a sender: either a number you've purchased, or an **alphanumeric
sender ID**: up to 11 characters, shown to the recipient in place of a number.

```typescript
export default config({
	sms: { provider: "smsworks", default: { from: "POSTBOI", country: "GB" } },
})
```

In the UK alphanumeric sender IDs are free and need no registration, which makes SMS setup
about as light as email. Two things to know: they are **one-way** (a recipient cannot reply
to one, so use a purchased number for conversations), and they must look like your brand,
because generic IDs get filtered.

In the US neither applies: sending needs 10DLC brand and campaign registration first, which
takes weeks and is arranged with your provider, not here.

## Cost, and message length

SMS is billed per **segment**, not per message. A GSM-7 message fits 160 characters in one
segment, then 153 per segment after that. A single character outside GSM-7 (an emoji, a
curly quote, an em dash) switches the whole message to UCS-2, where a segment is **70**
characters:

```typescript
import Mock from "postboi/sms-mock"

const text = new Mock()
await text.send({ to: "+447788223344", message: "…" })
text.last?.segments // { count: 1, encoding: "gsm7", units: 17 }
```

That's usually the difference between one segment and three, so it's worth knowing before
you paste in a “smart quote”.

### Checking a text before you send it

`sms.validate()` takes the same options as `sms()` and sends nothing. It answers with the
numbers as they'd be sent, the encoding and segment count, the characters that forced
UCS-2, and **every** problem a send would refuse, where a send stops at the first:

```typescript
import { sms } from "postboi"

const check = await sms.validate({ to: "07788 223344", message: "Your code is “4291”" })
// {
//   ok: true, provider: "twilio", to: ["+447788223344"],
//   encoding: "ucs2", segments: 1, units: 19,
//   ucs2_characters: ["“", "”"],
//   issues: [],
// }
if (!check.ok) console.log(check.issues) // [{ code: "invalid_number", message: "…" }, …]
```

Each issue's `code` is the one a send would throw. Beyond the checks every send makes
(a recipient, a sender, a message, scheduling, readable numbers), it reports what the
provider in particular would refuse: several recipients on a provider that takes one per
request, Twilio scheduling without a Messaging Service, and an alphanumeric sender longer
than 11 characters or with anything but letters, digits, spaces and `+ - _ &`. With a
[failover chain](#failing-over) the answer is the first provider's, and `chain` holds the
check against each. Every provider has the same `validate()`, and it's free: nothing is
asked of the provider, so whether a sender is registered or a number reachable is still
only known by sending.

## Scheduling

Where a provider supports it, `scheduled_at` takes a `Date`, an ISO string or a relative
[duration](/scheduling):

```typescript
await sms({ to: "+447788223344", message: "…", scheduled_at: { hours: 2 } })
```

Providers that **can't** schedule reject the send rather than delivering immediately. A
text meant for Tuesday arriving now is worse than an error, and silent.

## Pictures, expiry and provider options

Three more options, each carried only where the provider can, and refused before any
request where it can't. [`sms.validate()`](#checking-a-text-before-you-send-it) lists all
three.

```typescript
await sms({
	to: "+15550001111",
	message: "Your order is on its way",
	media: ["https://example.com/parcel.png"], // MMS
	validity: 600, // give up after ten minutes
	provider_options: { MessageIntent: "notifications" }, // straight into the request
})
```

| Option | Carried by | Refused elsewhere as |
| ------ | ---------- | -------------------- |
| `media`, public http(s) URLs | Twilio, Telnyx, Plivo and Bandwidth, up to 10 | `media_not_supported` |
| `validity`, seconds the provider keeps trying | Twilio (1 to 36000), Vonage (20 to 604800), Plivo (5 to 10799), Sinch (up to three days) | `validity_not_supported` |
| `provider_options` | Every provider but Amazon SNS | `provider_options_not_supported` |

`validity` is for the text that's worthless late, such as a one-time code. `provider_options`
are the provider's own parameter names, written as its reference spells them (Twilio's are
PascalCase), and checked for one thing only: they can't set a field the send already sets,
such as `Body` or `to` (`reserved_provider_option`). The rest is between you and the
provider.

## Sending many

Pass an array. Each message gets its own result, so one failure never loses the rest:

```typescript
const results = await sms([
	{ to: "+447788223344", message: "one" },
	{ to: "+353871234567", message: "two" },
])

for (const result of results) {
	if (!result.ok) console.error(result.index, result.error.message)
}
```

## Providers

| Provider | Import | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The SMS Works | `postboi/smsworks` | UK, bills only for delivered messages |
| PureSMS | `postboi/puresms` | UK, flat-rate pay-as-you-go, hosted in the EU |
| Twilio | `postboi/twilio` | Global, and automatic RCS upgrade via a Messaging Service |
| Telnyx | `postboi/telnyx` | Global on its own carrier network, usually the cheapest US route |
| Vonage | `postboi/vonage` | Global, through the Messages API |
| Plivo | `postboi/plivo` | Global, with MMS in the US and Canada and Powerpack number pools |
| Sinch | `postboi/sinch` | Global, scheduling, and up to 1000 recipients a request |
| Bandwidth | `postboi/bandwidth` | US, on its own carrier network, with MMS |
| Amazon SNS | `postboi/sns` | Already on AWS |

**SMS always names its provider**. There is no inferring it from credentials:

```bash
POSTBOI_SMS_PROVIDER=twilio
```

`bunx postboi init --sms` writes that line for you, and `bunx postboi doctor` checks it:
that each provider it names is one, that their credentials are set, and that
`POSTBOI_SMS_FROM` is a sender carriers take. Nothing is sent, so a wrong token still
passes; only a send can find that out. The reason it isn't optional is the same
one behind [development interception](#development-sends-nothing): a text costs money and reaches a real
handset, and the credentials that would do the guessing aren't evidence you meant to send
one. `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` is set by anything near AWS; `TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID` and
`TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN` are the Twilio SDK's zero-argument defaults, so a project using Voice or
Verify already has them. [WhatsApp](/whatsapp) works the same way. Push and chat, which cost
nothing and can be recalled, do infer.

**Telnyx** takes `TELNYX_API_KEY`, and `TELNYX_MESSAGING_PROFILE_ID` when you send from a
number pool or an alphanumeric sender (the profile then picks the number, so `from` can be
left out). It schedules with `scheduled_at`. **Vonage** takes `VONAGE_API_KEY` with either
`VONAGE_API_SECRET` or, for a number linked to a Vonage application, `VONAGE_APPLICATION_ID`
and `VONAGE_PRIVATE_KEY` (Vonage refuses the key and secret for those), plus
`VONAGE_REGION` for `api-eu`, `api-us` or `api-ap`. It can't schedule, so a `scheduled_at`
is refused rather than sent now. Both send one recipient per request, as Twilio does: pass
an array of sends to reach several people. Their receipts and replies arrive at `receive()`; see
[delivery receipts](#delivery-receipts).

**Plivo** takes `PLIVO_AUTH_ID` and `PLIVO_AUTH_TOKEN`, and `PLIVO_POWERPACK_UUID` to send from
a Powerpack's number pool instead of a fixed `from`. Several recipients go in one request.
**Sinch** takes `SINCH_SERVICE_PLAN_ID` and `SINCH_API_TOKEN`, `SINCH_REGION` outside the US
(`eu`, `au`, `br` or `ca`), and `SINCH_CALLBACK_URL` for where its delivery reports go. It
schedules, and the first of a send's `tags` rides along as Sinch's `client_reference`.
**Bandwidth** takes `BANDWIDTH_ACCOUNT_ID`, `BANDWIDTH_USERNAME`, `BANDWIDTH_PASSWORD` and
`BANDWIDTH_MESSAGING_APPLICATION_ID`. Several numbers in one Bandwidth request make a group
message where everyone sees everyone, so a send carries one recipient. Bandwidth documents no
limit on how long a text is tried, so `validity` is refused there; pass
`provider_options: { expiration }` to set it yourself. Plivo and Bandwidth can't schedule.
Receipts from these three aren't read by `receive()` yet; point them at your own handler.

Construct one directly instead of using the environment, exactly like an email provider:

```typescript
import Twilio from "postboi/twilio"

const text = new Twilio({
	account_sid: process.env.TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID,
	auth_token: process.env.TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN,
	default: { from: "+15550001111" },
})

await text.send({ to: "+447788223344", message: "…" })
```

## Failing over

Name more than one provider and `sms()` tries them in order:

```bash
POSTBOI_SMS_PROVIDER=twilio,puresms
```

```typescript
// or in postboi.config.ts
export default config({ sms: { provider: ["twilio", "puresms"] } })
```

Each text goes to the first. It moves to the next only when the first **refused it for
a reason that is about that provider**, which every error now says in `reason`:

| `reason` | Means | Fails over |
| -------- | ----- | ---------- |
| `auth` | Bad credentials, or no permission | Yes |
| `rate_limited` | Too many requests, after any `retries` | Yes |
| `sender` | The From can't send this (unregistered, not SMS-capable) | Yes |
| `account` | No balance, a region switched off, a trial account's limits | Yes |
| `recipient` | Not a number anyone can reach | No |
| `compliance` | They opted out, or the content was blocked | No |
| `request` | Anything else wrong with the request | No |

It **never** fails over after a timeout, a dropped connection or a 5xx. Those are
`outcome_unknown`: the first provider may already have sent the text, and the second would
send it again. When every provider refuses, the error is a `FailoverError` whose `errors`
holds each one's answer in order; everything else on it is the last provider's. A batch
fails over message by message, so only the texts the first provider refused go to the
second.

- **Every provider in the chain needs its credentials**, or the send fails with
  `missing_env` naming the one that is missing. A backup you find out is broken at the moment
  you need it is not a backup.
- **`options` in the config file belong to the first provider.** The others read env only,
  because one flat bag can't say which of two providers sharing an option name it was for.
- **The chain shares one sender** (`POSTBOI_SMS_FROM`, or `from` on the send), and a number
  registered with Twilio can't be sent from through PureSMS. An alphanumeric sender both
  accept works; so does a Twilio Messaging Service, which picks its own number. Where each
  provider needs a different number, build the chain yourself, below.
- **Receipts come from each provider separately**, so a chain needs each one's
  [webhook](#delivery-receipts) set up, each naming its provider.

To test a chain without a provider, script a mock's failures. `fail` takes one failure
for every send, or a list read one entry per send (`null` succeeds):

```typescript
import MockSms from "postboi/sms-mock"

const first = new MockSms({ fail: [{ reason: "sender" }, null] }) // refused once, then fine
const flaky = new MockSms({ fail: { outcome_unknown: true } }) // never fails over
```

The same `fail` works on the WhatsApp, push and chat mocks.

`fails_over(error)` from the package root is the same test, for a chain of your own:

```typescript
import { fails_over } from "postboi"
import Twilio from "postboi/twilio"
import PureSms from "postboi/puresms"

const chain = [
	new Twilio({ account_sid, auth_token, default: { from: "+15550001111" } }),
	new PureSms({ api_key, default: { from: "ACME" } }),
]

async function text(message: { to: string; message: string }) {
	for (const [i, provider] of chain.entries()) {
		try {
			return await provider.send(message)
		} catch (error) {
			if (i === chain.length - 1 || !fails_over(error)) throw error
		}
	}
}
```

## RCS: an upgrade, not a channel

RCS is the carrier-native successor to SMS: branded sender, delivery receipts, long
messages billed once instead of per segment. Since iOS 18.1 it covers both platforms, and
**with Twilio it needs no code at all**. Add an RCS-capable sender to a
[Messaging Service](https://www.twilio.com/docs/messaging/services) and put its SID in the
environment. Twilio routes each message by device capability with automatic SMS fallback,
and your sending code doesn't change:

```bash
# .env
TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=MG…   # has an RCS sender attached
```

```typescript
import { sms } from "postboi"

await sms({ to: "+447788223344", message: "…" }) // RCS if the device can, SMS if not
```

Constructing the provider yourself instead? Instances don't read the environment, so pass
`messaging_service_sid` to the `new Twilio({ … })` constructor.

Worth knowing before you switch it on:

- **Sender registration is console-side**: brand verification through your provider, with
  a one-time onboarding fee and a lead time of days to weeks.
- **Pricing is parity-to-higher for short messages** (RCS carries carrier fees too), but a
  message over 160 characters bills **once** rather than per segment. The crossover where
  RCS gets cheaper than cheap UK SMS is around 3 segments.
- **Which rail delivered** arrives on Twilio's status callbacks, not the send response,
  because the message is queued before the routing decision happens.

## Delivery receipts

A send response says the provider *accepted* the message. Whether it reached the handset
comes later, and each provider reports it its own way. All of them land in the same
normalized [webhook events](/webhooks#text-messages), with `channel: "sms"` and the number in
`phone`:

- **The SMS Works pushes.** Delivery reports go to one account-wide URL, so point it at
  `receive()` like an email provider: on the dashboard, **Delivery Reports → Webhook
  Configuration**, paste your endpoint with a token you make up as `?token=…`, and set the
  same value as `SMSWORKS_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. There is no signature scheme to verify. The
  token is compared timing-safe, and that is honestly weaker than the HMAC most email
  providers offer. Their delivery reports come from three published source addresses
  (listed on their [developer page](https://thesmsworks.co.uk/developers)) if you want a
  second check in front.
- **Telnyx pushes Ed25519-signed events** to the messaging profile's webhook URL (or the
  provider's `webhook_url`). Set `TELNYX_WEBHOOK_SECRET` to the account's *public* key
  (Mission Control → Keys & Credentials), and point the URL at `receive()` with
  `{ provider: "telnyx" }`. Timestamps older than five minutes are refused, as Telnyx asks.
- **Vonage signs with a JWT.** Point the application's Status and Inbound URLs at
  `receive()` with `{ provider: "vonage" }`, and set `VONAGE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` to the
  signature secret from the dashboard's settings. The token's `payload_hash` ties it to the
  body as delivered.
- **PureSMS pushes signed receipts.** Add your endpoint under **Settings → Webhooks** with
  a secret, and set the same value as `PURESMS_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. Each request is signed with
  HMAC-SHA256 over its timestamp and body. A receipt doesn't name the recipient's number,
  so `phone` is unset on one; the first tag the send carried comes back in `tags`.
- **Twilio pushes or is polled.** Its status callbacks go wherever the send said, so
  construct the provider with `status_callback` (or set `TWILIO_STATUS_CALLBACK`, or the
  Messaging Service's own setting) and point `receive()` at it with
  `{ provider: "twilio" }`. Requests are signed with your auth token, which
  `TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN` already holds. Twilio signs the URL it called, so behind a proxy
  or a tunnel pass that URL, query string included, as `receive(request, { url })`.
  `webhook()` answers Twilio with empty TwiML, which is what an inbound message's
  webhook expects back. With nothing set up,
  `poll({ provider: "twilio" })` reads the same receipts from the Message resource and
  needs no public endpoint. See [polling](/webhooks#providers-that-dont-push-poll).

```typescript
// src/routes/webhooks/sms/+server.ts — the same one-liner as an email webhook
import { webhook } from "postboi/kit"

export const POST = webhook(
	async (event) => {
		if (event.type === "delivered") await mark_delivered(event.message_id)
		if (event.type === "failed") console.warn(`${event.phone}: ${event.bounce?.detail}`)
	},
	{ provider: "smsworks" }
)
```

A text that reached the handset is `delivered`; one the carrier gave up on
(`UNDELIVERABLE`, `REJECTED`, `EXPIRED`) is `failed`, with the carrier's code and words in
`bounce.detail`. The SMS Works also says whether a failure is permanent, so
`bounce.category` is `"hard"` or `"soft"` rather than the `"unknown"` a Twilio failure
carries; a `SENT` still carrying a temporary error is the carrier retrying, and arrives as
`delayed`. `message_id` is the per-message id. For a [batch send](#sending-many) the id
`send()` returned was the batch's, which each report carries as `batchid` in `raw`.
A message the provider is still holding (`SCHEDULED`) is never an event. Test the whole
path without a tunnel: `mock_request({ provider: "smsworks", type: "failed" })` builds a
signed-in delivery report.

## Opt-outs

Someone who texts **STOP** back has opted out, and the law in most places says so
before any provider does. The keyword list is the one every carrier recognises (`STOP`,
`STOPALL`, `UNSUBSCRIBE`, `CANCEL`, `END`, `QUIT`, and `ARRET` for Canada), and it is a
whole-message test: `stop.` opts out, `please stop texting me` is a person to reply to.

Two more keywords matter. **START** (or `UNSTOP`) is the same person opting back in, so
the suppression you wrote for their STOP should go. **HELP** (or `INFO`) asks who you are
and how to stop, and US carriers expect an answer to it. `YES` is deliberately not an
opt-in here, although Twilio counts it as one: it is also how people answer "Reply YES to
confirm".

Every inbound text becomes one event, for the number that sent it:

| They text | Event | `keyword` |
| --------- | ----- | --------- |
| STOP and friends | `unsubscribed` | `"stop"` |
| START, UNSTOP | `resubscribed` | `"start"` |
| HELP, INFO | `received`, with their words in `body.text` | `"help"` |
| Anything else | `received`, with their words in `body.text` | unset |

`channel` is `"sms"` or `"whatsapp"`, whichever they replied over, and `phone` is their
number. On a `received`, `message_id` is the send they replied to where the provider knows
it, and the text's own id otherwise. These arrive from:

- **`receive()` on Twilio**, where Twilio's own Advanced Opt-Out verdict (`OptOutType`)
  is used when it gave one, and `poll()` on Twilio, which reads the same Message resource
  as its receipts and matches the words itself.
- **The SMS Works' reply webhook**, configured per reply number or keyword on their
  dashboard, at the same endpoint as its [delivery reports](#delivery-receipts).
- **PureSMS's inbound messages**, at the same endpoint as its receipts.
- **`receive()` on Meta**, for [WhatsApp via the Cloud API](/whatsapp#delivery-receipts-and-replies).
- **`sms_keyword(text)`** and **`is_opt_out(text)`**, from the package root, are the same
  test on its own, for an inbound webhook you already handle.

```typescript
import { poll } from "postboi/webhooks"
import { is_opt_out, sms_keyword, mail, sms } from "postboi"

const { events } = await poll({ provider: "twilio", cursor: saved })
for (const event of events) {
	if (event.type === "unsubscribed" && event.phone) {
		await mail.suppressions.add({ phone: event.phone, channel: event.channel })
	}
	if (event.type === "received" && event.keyword === "help" && event.channel === "sms" && event.phone) {
		await sms({ to: event.phone, message: "Acme alerts. Reply STOP to opt out." })
	}
}

is_opt_out("STOP") // true
is_opt_out("Can you stop by tomorrow?") // false
sms_keyword("unstop") // "start"
```

On the [Postboi provider](/provider#suppressions), the suppression list is per channel
and a polled STOP lands on it without the loop above: the number is suppressed for
the channel it replied over, and `mail.suppressions.all({ channel: "sms" })` shows it.
Twilio's own opt-out handling still applies where it exists (US and Canadian long
codes block the number at their end); this is how the fact reaches *you*. An
alphanumeric sender ID can't be replied to, so a STOP never arrives. That's one more reason
the [sender](#sender) section suggests a number for anything conversational.

## Hooks

[Hooks](/hooks) run on every channel, so narrow on `channel` before reading fields that
only one of them has:

```typescript
export default config({
	hooks: {
		before: {
			send: ({ channel, message }) => {
				if (channel === "sms") console.log(message.to, message.message)
				if (channel === "email") console.log(message.subject)
			},
		},
	},
})
```

**Runnable examples:** [`examples/scripts/sms.ts`](https://github.com/postboi-mail/postboi/tree/main/examples/scripts/sms.ts) is the whole
channel in a file you can run: one text, a batch, and the errors worth catching. Every
framework app's `POST /notify` route sends `sms()`, `whatsapp()` and `slack()` side by
side; the [SvelteKit one](https://github.com/postboi-mail/postboi/tree/main/examples/sveltekit-provider-postboi) is the shortest read.
